\section{Preface}

These notes grew out of a one-semester course. They assume a first course in quantum
information theory and aim at the reader who wants to understand \emph{why} the standard
constructions work, not only what they are.\footnote{Comments and corrections are
welcome; see the course page.} The notes are organized in parts, each part holding a
few chapters, each chapter a few sections, so that a reader can study one chapter at a
time.

\subsection{What we'll cover here}

We begin with the fundamental theory of protecting information against noise, continue
with stabilizer codes, and end with the surface code and its friends. Throughout we use
the formalism recalled in \ref{sec:recap}.

\subsection{Recommended literature}

The standard references are the textbooks \cite{manual:{M. A. Nielsen and I. L. Chuang, Quantum Computation and Quantum Information, Cambridge University Press (2000)}} and \cite{manual:{D. Gottesman, Stabilizer Codes and Quantum Error Correction, PhD thesis, Caltech (1997)}}, and the surveys \cite{manual:{B. M. Terhal, Quantum error correction for quantum memories, Rev. Mod. Phys. 87, 307 (2015)},manual:{D. A. Lidar and T. A. Brun (eds.), Quantum Error Correction, Cambridge University Press (2013)},manual:{J. Roffe, Quantum error correction: an introductory guide, Contemp. Phys. 60, 226 (2019)}}.

\part{Fundamental theory of quantum error correction}

\section{Protecting information against noise}\label{sec:fndthry}

Consider a quantum system that is subject to noise. We would like to store a quantum
state in it for a while and recover that state later. The idea of
\term{error correction} is to store the state not directly but in a cleverly chosen
subspace, the \term{code}, such that the effect of the noise can be undone.

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics{cycle.svg}
  \caption[alt=The cycle]{The error-correction cycle: a logical state is encoded into a
  physical system, noise acts, and a recovery operation restores the logical state.}
  \label{figure:cycle}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Encoding logical information on a physical system}\label{sec:encoding}

\begin{defterm}{code}\label{topic:code}\aliasterm{codes}
  A \emph{code} is a subspace \(\mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{H}\) of the Hilbert space
  of a physical system, together with an identification of \(\mathcal{C}\) with the
  logical Hilbert space \(\mathcal{H}_L\) through an isometry
  \(V\colon \mathcal{H}_L \to \mathcal{H}\).
\end{defterm}

\begin{defterm}{error correction}\label{topic:ec}
  \emph{Error correction} is the task of finding, for a given noise channel
  \(\mathcal{N}\) and a given \term{code}, a recovery channel \(\mathcal{R}\) such that
  \(\mathcal{R}\circ\mathcal{N}\circ V = V\) up to a trivial map on the logical system.
\end{defterm}

The channel \(\mathcal{N}\) is described by Kraus operators \(\{E_k\}\),
\begin{align}
  \mathcal{N}(\rho) &= \sum_k E_k \rho E_k^\dagger,
  \label{eq:kraus}\\
  \sum_k E_k^\dagger E_k &= \mathbb{1}.
  \label{eq:tp}
\end{align}
Condition \ref{eq:tp} says that the channel is trace preserving. We write
\(P = V V^\dagger\) for the projector onto the code space.

\subsection{Criteria for quantum error correction}\label{sec:criteria}

\begin{theorem}[Knill--Laflamme conditions]\label{thm:kl}
  Let \(\mathcal{C}\) be a \term{code} with projector \(P\), and let \(\{E_k\}\) be a set
  of error operators. There exists a recovery channel \(\mathcal{R}\) correcting every
  channel whose Kraus operators lie in the span of the \(E_k\) if and only if
  \begin{align}
    P E_k^\dagger E_\ell P = c_{k\ell}\, P
    \label{eq:kl}
  \end{align}
  for some Hermitian matrix \((c_{k\ell})\).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[*thm:kl]
  We prove the easier direction, that the condition is necessary. Suppose a recovery
  channel \(\mathcal{R}\) with Kraus operators \(\{R_j\}\) exists. For any code state
  \(|\psi\rangle\), the recovered state must equal \(|\psi\rangle\), so
  \(R_j E_k |\psi\rangle = \lambda_{jk} |\psi\rangle\) for every code state. Taking inner
  products and summing over \(j\) gives \ref{eq:kl} with
  \(c_{k\ell} = \sum_j \overline{\lambda_{jk}}\lambda_{j\ell}\). The converse is left to
  the exercises.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
  The matrix \((c_{k\ell})\) can be diagonalized by choosing a new basis of error
  operators; the conditions are then simply \(P E_k^\dagger E_\ell P = \delta_{k\ell}
  d_k P\). Errors with \(d_k = 0\) never happen on the code space.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:detect}
  A set of errors \(\{E_k\}\) is \emph{detectable} by the code \(\mathcal{C}\) if and
  only if \(P E_k P = c_k P\) for all \(k\).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[*lem:detect]
  Apply \ref{thm:kl} with the identity as one of the error operators. \ref{lem:detect}
  is thus a special case of the general criterion.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Error-detecting codes}

Detection is weaker than correction: we learn that something went wrong, but not what.
In many settings, for instance in \emph{post-selected} experiments, this is already
useful. As noted in \ref{sec:criteria}, detectability is a statement about the
projected error operators alone.

\begin{itemize}
\item A code detects an error \(E\) if \(PEP \propto P\).
\item A code corrects a set of errors if it detects every product \(E_k^\dagger E_\ell\).
\item The trivial code \(\mathcal{C} = \mathcal{H}\) detects nothing.
\end{itemize}

\section{Encoding information on multiple subsystems}\label{sec:multi}

Most codes of interest live on \(n\) physical qubits, and the errors of interest act on
few of them at a time. This chapter makes that intuition precise.

\subsection{Weights of errors}

The \emph{weight} of an operator \(E\) on \(n\) qubits is the number of qubits on which it
acts non-trivially. The Pauli operators \(\{\mathbb{1}, X, Y, Z\}^{\otimes n}\) form a
basis, and every error can be expanded in it.

\subsection{Code distance}\label{sec:distance}

\begin{definition}\label{def:distance}
  The \emph{distance} \(d\) of a code is the smallest weight of a Pauli operator \(E\)
  that is not detectable, that is, with \(PEP \not\propto P\).
\end{definition}

A code of distance \(d\) corrects all errors of weight at most \(\lfloor (d-1)/2
\rfloor\), by \ref{lem:detect}. We write \([[n, k, d]]\) for a code on \(n\) qubits
encoding \(k\) logical qubits with distance \(d\).

\begin{quote}
  \text{A code is a way of writing a message so that the message survives the loss of some of
  its letters.}
\end{quote}

\part{Stabilizer codes}

\section{Qubit stabilizer codes}\label{sec:stabilizer}

The \term{stabilizer formalism} is the workhorse of quantum error correction; nearly
every code in practical use is a stabilizer code.

\subsection{The stabilizer formalism}\label{sec:recap}

\begin{defterm}{stabilizer formalism}\label{topic:stab}
  A \emph{stabilizer group} is an abelian subgroup \(\mathcal{S}\) of the \(n\)-qubit
  Pauli group not containing \(-\mathbb{1}\). The \emph{stabilizer code} it defines is
  the joint \(+1\) eigenspace of all of its elements.
\end{defterm}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:dim}
  A stabilizer group with \(n - k\) independent generators defines a code of dimension
  \(2^k\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[**prop:dim]
  Each independent generator halves the dimension of the joint eigenspace.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Correcting errors in stabilizer codes}

A Pauli error \(E\) either commutes or anticommutes with each generator; the pattern of
anticommutations is the \emph{syndrome} of \(E\). Errors with distinct syndromes are
distinguishable, and the decoder's job is to guess the most likely error given the
syndrome.\footnote{For most codes of interest, optimal decoding is computationally
hard, which is why so much effort goes into clever approximate decoders.}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Measure every generator of \(\mathcal{S}\).
\item Compute the syndrome.
\item Apply the correction the decoder suggests.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Some essential stabilizer codes}\label{sec:examples}

\subsection{The repetition code}

The three-qubit repetition code is stabilized by \(Z_1 Z_2\) and \(Z_2 Z_3\). It corrects
one bit flip but no phase flip, and so it is not a quantum code in the full sense; it is
nonetheless the right place to start.

\subsection{The Shor code}

Concatenating the bit-flip repetition code with its phase-flip counterpart gives the
nine-qubit code of Shor \cite{manual:{P. W. Shor, Scheme for reducing decoherence in quantum computer memory, Phys. Rev. A 52, R2493 (1995)}}, the first quantum error-correcting code. See also the
discussion of the distance in \ref{sec:distance} and the original
\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9705052}{thesis of Gottesman}.
